    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d534">

                <pb facs="0838" n="828" xml:id="pg828"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.20 Missions/4–2546: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-04-25T11:00:00+03:00">April 25, 1946—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 2:37 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1327. Military Advisory Group to China is subject. Following is translation of
                        Vyshinski’s<anchor n="41" xml:id="d534-f1"/> letter of April 19 to Kennan’s
                    letter of March 2 to Molotov<anchor n="42" xml:id="d534-f2"/> (ReEmbtel 626,
                    March 2<anchor n="43" xml:id="d534-f3"/>): <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In connection with Mr. Kennan’s letter of March 2, to the Minister for
                            Foreign Affairs of the USSR, V. M. Molotov, in which it is stated that
                            the Govt of the United States intends soon to begin negotiations with
                            the Chinese Government regarding the establishment [of] a group of
                            United States military advisers in China in the near future, I submit
                            the following:</p>
                        <p>The Soviet Govt is aware that American military advisers have already
                            been in China for a long time and it would therefore be grateful for
                            clarification as to whether the negotiations have as their purpose the
                            concluding of an agreement relative to the group of American military
                            advisers already in China or whether the negotiations mentioned concern
                            the establishment of a new group of military advisers. The Soviet
                            Government would also appreciate information regarding the number
                            contemplated for the group of the American military advisers in
                            China.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Smith</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d534-f1" n="41" xml:id="d534-f1-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, Soviet Deputy Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d534-f2" n="42" xml:id="d534-f2-content">
                <p>Vyacheslav Mikhailovich Molotov, Soviet Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d534-f3" n="43" xml:id="d534-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported transmission of letter dated March 2 to Mr. Molotov
                    giving information contained in Department’s telegram No. 342, February 27, 7
                    p.m., <ref target="#pg824">p. 824</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
